/*
    Two questions about the octave buttons, answered on the keys.

    Independent blocks have not worked, and I have guessed at why twice without
    checking. There are only two possibilities and this separates them.

    Load it on BOTH blocks, then press an octave button on ONE of them.

      keys 0-10   the value of config item 4 on this block
                  key 5 lit means zero, key 6 means +1, key 4 means -1, and so on

      key 12      flashes white when this block's own octave-down button was pressed
      key 14      flashes white when this block's own octave-up button was pressed

    What to look for.

    If the bar moves on BOTH blocks when you press a button on ONE, the firmware is
    syncing the octave across the cluster underneath the program. Nothing the program
    does can make the blocks independent while the octave lives in config item 4, and
    the fix is for LumiPaint to keep its own octave and stop using item 4 for it.

    If the bar moves only on the block you pressed, the firmware is not syncing, the
    sharing is entirely the program's own doing, and independence is simply a matter of
    not sending it - which is what the toggle already tries to do, so something else is
    wrong and I want to know that too.

    Whether keys 12 and 14 flash answers the other question: whether the buttons reach
    the program at all, or are handled in firmware where the program never sees them.
*/

int flashDown;
int flashUp;

void initialise()
{
    flashDown = 0;
    flashUp = 0;

    setUseDefaultKeyHandler(true, false);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(4, true, true);
    setLocalConfigItemRange(4, -4, 6);
    setLocalConfigActiveState(36, true, true);
}

void handleButtonDown(int index)
{
    if (index == 1) { flashDown = 25; }
    if (index == 2) { flashUp = 25; }
}

void repaint()
{
    for (int k = 0; k < 24; k = k + 1) { fillPixel(0xFF080808, k, 0); }

    int octave = getLocalConfig(4);
    int position = 5 + octave;

    if (position < 0) { position = 0; }
    if (position > 10) { position = 10; }

    // The zero mark stays lit dimly, so the bar can be read against it.
    fillPixel(0xFF203040, 5, 0);
    fillPixel(0xFF20C040, position, 0);

    if (flashDown > 0)
    {
        flashDown = flashDown - 1;
        fillPixel(0xFFFFFFFF, 12, 0);
    }

    if (flashUp > 0)
    {
        flashUp = flashUp - 1;
        fillPixel(0xFFFFFFFF, 14, 0);
    }
}
